# svGolf PINNED scoring flow, stage 1 of 2: CANONICALIZATION. Do not edit: a
# change here is a change to every score. score.py fills the @NAME@
# placeholders and records this file's sha256 (`canon_script_sha256`) in every
# result.
#
#   @SOURCES@  read_verilog -sv lines, one per (source-gated) source file
#   @TOP@      the task's top module
#   @CHPARAM@  " -chparam <name> <value>" per parameter override (may be empty)
#   @RTLIL@    output file: the canonical RTLIL that stage 2 (svgolf.ys) reads
#
# The frontend's design, elaborated at the scoring point, processes lowered
# (`proc`) and flattened (what `synth -flatten` does first anyway), with every
# source location (`src`), every original hierarchical name (`hdlname`) and
# every non-port name removed: `rename -hide` makes them private and
# `rename -enumerate` numbers them. Stage 2 reads this text in a FRESH Yosys
# process. Why two processes: Yosys's results depend on process-global state
# (its IdString table, whose interning order the frontend's history of names
# such as `$and$design.sv:42$17` decides), so the same circuit written with a
# different layout, different names or a different item order synthesizes to
# a different netlist when frontend and synthesis share one process, even
# after the names are stripped (measured: fdiv16 gave 9 scores over 34
# variants of one design). A fresh process reading the canonical RTLIL makes
# the score a function of the circuit's expression structure only (README.md,
# "Scoring").
@SOURCES@
hierarchy -check -top @TOP@@CHPARAM@
proc
flatten -noscopeinfo
hierarchy -top @TOP@
setattr -unset src
setattr -mod -unset src
setattr -unset hdlname
setattr -mod -unset hdlname
rename -hide
rename -enumerate
write_rtlil @RTLIL@
